# The .ko-agent-sandbox/egress/rule of a project that runs `pulumi up` against AWS: the CLI's
# download host, then the state backend and each cloud API the stack writes to as opaque tunnels —
# no wildcards, one host each.
# This stack is in us-east-1 with VPC, EC2, ECS, Aurora, CloudWatch, IAM, Secrets Manager, and
# stack secrets encrypted by KMS (--secrets-provider awskms://...). Launch with the credentials
# forwarded: --env=PULUMI_ACCESS_TOKEN --env=AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID --env=AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY;
# SECURITY.md, "Credential theft", is what that costs, and says to forward a read-only role's
# temporary credential — AWS_SESSION_TOKEN with the two above — when the session is to
# `pulumi preview`. IAM then refuses a mutation whoever requests it: `pulumi up`, which may still
# start and compute its update, the AWS CLI, or the program's own SDK call. An action a
# provider's preview needs but the role lacks fails the same way. The state backend is a second
# authority: a preview reads state and `--refresh` writes it, so PULUMI_ACCESS_TOKEN's permission
# set decides that separately; whether a read-only token's `--refresh` works must be tested
# against the backend in use, not assumed.
# get.pulumi.com serves the CLI, and the plugins GitHub releases lack
allow https://get.pulumi.com/ read
allow https://api.pulumi.com/ tunnel                      # state backend; not for a local login
allow https://sts.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/ tunnel         # the AWS provider's first call, always
allow https://ec2.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/ tunnel         # EC2 and VPC
allow https://ecs.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/ tunnel
allow https://rds.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/ tunnel         # Aurora
allow https://monitoring.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/ tunnel  # CloudWatch metrics and alarms
allow https://logs.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/ tunnel        # CloudWatch Logs
allow https://iam.amazonaws.com/ tunnel                   # global, no region
allow https://secretsmanager.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/ tunnel
allow https://kms.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/ tunnel         # the stack's secrets provider
